using Spectrum.Models;
using System.Collections.Generic;

namespace Spectrum.Services
{
    /// <summary>The color glossary shown from Analyze's Help flyout. Content only —
    /// the flyout searches and scrolls it; nothing here is palette-dependent.</summary>
    public static class GlossaryService
    {
        public static IReadOnlyList<GlossaryTerm> Terms { get; } = new List<GlossaryTerm>
        {
            new("AA (WCAG)",
                "WCAG's practical-minimum compliance level for body text contrast. Requires a 4.5:1 ratio. Hitting AA means readable, not necessarily visible in the way you want a CTA to be visible."),
            new("AAA (WCAG)",
                "WCAG's enhanced contrast level. Requires 7:1 for body text. The target for anything the eye needs to find first — primary CTAs, error messages, focus rings."),
            new("Achromatopsia",
                "Full monochromacy: no chromatic perception at all. Extraordinarily rare (~1 in 30,000). Designs that use redundant non-color signals — shape, text, lightness — survive achromatopsia automatically."),
            new("Additive color",
                "The mixing model for light: combining wavelengths adds them. Red + green light = yellow; red + green + blue = white. Every screen, projector and camera sensor uses this. Primaries: RGB."),
            new("Analogous",
                "Hues nearby on the wheel, typically within ~30° of each other. Reads as a coherent family, useful when you want a cohesive feel rather than contrast. The risk is too much cohesion: nothing has authority."),
            new("Chroma",
                "How colorful something is: the C in OKLCH. At chroma 0 every hue collapses to gray. At full chroma you are at the saturated edge of the color space — practically capped around 0.4 in OKLCH for sRGB."),
            new("Chromaticity",
                "A two-dimensional coordinate that specifies a color's hue and saturation, stripped of brightness. The CIE 1931 horseshoe diagram plots every chromaticity a healthy human eye can perceive; gamut triangles are drawn on it so coverage is legible at a glance."),
            new("Color blindness (CVD)",
                "Color vision deficiency: a missing or shifted cone photoreceptor type that collapses one of the brain's opponent axes. Affects ~8% of men and ~0.5% of women. The accommodation is not muting the palette — it is making sure hue is never the only signal carrying meaning."),
            new("Color constancy",
                "The brain's correction for changes in illumination: a banana looks yellow under sunlight, fluorescent and tungsten lighting even though the reflected wavelengths differ dramatically. Why white balance exists in photography and why monitors profile their white point."),
            new("Color harmony",
                "A set of hues arranged by a fixed geometric rule on the color wheel — Itten's inscribed shapes. The geometry is reliable (rotations are deterministic); whether a particular arrangement looks good is empirical, not law."),
            new("Color space",
                "A gamut plus a transfer curve plus a white point — the full triple needed to specify what numeric values mean as light. sRGB and Rec.709 share a gamut but are different spaces because their transfer curves differ."),
            new("Color wheel",
                "A circular arrangement of hues that encodes their cyclic relationships. Newton drew the first in 1672; Itten produced the canonical 12-color version still in use. The wheel encodes hue only — value and chroma live on perpendicular axes."),
            new("Complementary",
                "Two hues 180° apart on the color wheel. Maximum visual contrast: the eye cannot integrate the pair into a single channel because they max out the brain's opponent encoding in opposite directions."),
            new("Contrast ratio",
                "The ratio of relative luminance between two colors, written as X:1. WCAG's measurement of legibility — higher is more readable. 1:1 means the colors are identical; 21:1 is pure black on pure white."),
            new("Design token",
                "A named, platform-agnostic record of a design decision (a color, a spacing value, a typography step). Coined at Salesforce in 2014 by Snook & Anne. Tokens are the API between design intent and code — naming them well is the substance of a working design system."),
            new("Deuteranopia (deutan)",
                "The most common form of color vision deficiency (~6% of men). The M cone is absent or shifted; the red-green opponent axis collapses. Red and green at the same lightness become indistinguishable."),
            new("Display P3",
                "Apple's consumer-friendly variant of DCI-P3, introduced with the iPhone 7 in 2016. DCI-P3 primaries with sRGB's transfer curve and a D65 white point. Covers ~45% of visible chromaticities — the modern practical ceiling for web work."),
            new("Ecological Valence Theory",
                "Palmer & Schloss's 2010 model of color preference: you like a color in proportion to how much you like the average set of objects that color appears on. The most empirically successful framework in modern color preference research — color preferences are learned, not innate."),
            new("Gamut",
                "The 3D volume of colors a system can represent, bounded by the chromaticities of its primaries. sRGB covers ~35% of visible chromaticities; Display P3 ~45%; Rec.2020 ~75%. Anything outside the gamut is unreachable on that system."),
            new("Hex",
                "A six-digit hexadecimal RGB representation (#RRGGBB). The web's universal storage and exchange format — compact and exact, but it says nothing about perception or how a color relates to others."),
            new("HSL",
                "Hue, Saturation, Lightness — a readable color triplet, but its lightness is a geometric midpoint, not perceptual. The same numeric L can produce wildly different perceived brightnesses across hues, which is why ramps generated in HSL look uneven."),
            new("Hue",
                "Position on the color wheel, measured in degrees: 0° red, 60° yellow, 120° green, 180° cyan, 240° blue, 300° magenta. The H in HSL and OKLCH."),
            new("Lightness",
                "How light or dark a color is. The L in HSL and OKLCH — but the two L values mean different things. HSL's L is geometric (midpoint between full hue and its opposite); OKLCH's L is perceptual (matches the way your eye sees brightness)."),
            new("Luminance",
                "Perceived brightness — what your eye registers, weighted across red, green and blue by the way the human retina actually responds. Different from numeric lightness in HSL: yellow at L=70% is much more luminous than blue at the same L."),
            new("Monochromatic",
                "One hue, varied through value and chroma. Functions through value contrast alone — same color, lighter or darker. The safest harmony because hue contrast is replaced by value contrast, which the eye tolerates much better."),
            new("OKLCH",
                "A perceptually uniform color space with three axes: Lightness, Chroma, Hue. Equal numeric jumps in any axis produce equal perceived jumps, which is why OKLCH is the right space for shade ramps, gradient interpolation and contrast math."),
            new("Opponent process",
                "Hering's 1892 model (formalized by Hurvich & Jameson in 1957) of how the visual cortex re-encodes cone signals into three opponent axes: red-green, blue-yellow and light-dark. Why you can imagine a yellowish-red but not a reddish-green — and why CIE Lab and OKLCH use two chromatic axes."),
            new("Palette",
                "A small set of colors that form a coherent visual system — typically a hero, a couple of supports and a couple of neutrals. Five hexes is the canonical size: enough to build a UI, small enough to force commitment."),
            new("Perceptual uniformity",
                "The property of a color space where equal numeric distances correspond to equal perceived distances. OKLCH has it; HSL, RGB and hex do not. It is why OKLCH ramps look even and HSL ramps look uneven."),
            new("Protanopia (protan)",
                "Color vision deficiency where the L cone is absent or shifted (~1% of men). Red-green collapses similarly to deutan, with the added effect that pure reds darken visibly because the L cone contributes most of red's perceived brightness."),
            new("Rec.2020 (BT.2020)",
                "ITU-R's 2012 spec for ultra-high-definition broadcast and HDR streaming. Primaries push to near-monochromatic wavelengths; covers ~75% of visible chromaticities. Almost no consumer display can render it fully — it is a target the industry is building toward."),
            new("Reference token",
                "A token in the lowest tier of the hierarchy: a raw palette value (palette.blue.500) named only by its position on a scale. Never referenced by component code directly; it exists to be aliased by semantic system tokens above it."),
            new("Replication crisis",
                "The reckoning across psychology since ~2011 that many canonical findings do not replicate at original effect sizes under pre-registered testing. The Open Science Collaboration's 2015 paper estimated less than 40% of canonical psychology findings replicated. Several famous color-psychology claims fell into the gap."),
            new("Semantic token",
                "A token whose name encodes a role (color.action.primary, color.text.muted) rather than an appearance or hue. Sits in the system tier. The name stays stable across themes; the value swaps underneath. The architectural unit that lets multi-theme design systems work."),
            new("Simultaneous contrast",
                "A perceptual phenomenon where a color's appearance shifts based on what surrounds it — the visual system enhances differences along the opponent axes. The mechanism behind Albers's Interaction of Color, behind why yellow light produces purple shadows, and behind why the same hex can read differently on different backgrounds."),
            new("Split-complementary",
                "A base hue plus the two hues 30° on either side of its complement (offsets 0°, 150°, 210°). Keeps the contrast pop of a complementary pair without the dead-on-180° vibration."),
            new("Square (harmony)",
                "Four hues at 90° apart on the wheel — a square inscribed in Itten's circle. Almost never works at full chroma (too many competing voices), but useful as a starting generator for a smaller palette."),
            new("sRGB",
                "The standard color space (IEC 61966-2-1, 1999) every monitor and browser supports. Covers ~35% of human-visible chromaticities — the universal floor for web color, and for 25 years also the practical ceiling. Modern displays now reach further."),
            new("Subtractive color",
                "The mixing model for pigments: each pigment absorbs (subtracts) certain wavelengths. Cyan + magenta + yellow ≈ black. Print uses CMYK because pure CMY produces a muddy brown rather than a clean black. Primaries: CMY."),
            new("Tetradic",
                "Two complementary pairs offset by 60° — a rectangle inscribed in the wheel. Four hues, two opponent pairs. More than most palettes need; usually pruned to two or three for actual deployment."),
            new("Token tier",
                "A layer in the reference / system / component hierarchy most modern systems use to organize tokens. Reference holds raw values, system holds semantic aliases (per theme), component holds slot-specific overrides. Components reference system tokens; system tokens reference reference tokens — never the reverse."),
            new("Transfer curve",
                "The function that maps numeric color values to actual emitted light. sRGB's is roughly 2.2 gamma. Compresses encoding to spend more of the limited bit budget where the eye is most sensitive (the dark end), avoiding banding in shadows."),
            new("Triadic",
                "Three hues evenly spaced on the wheel, 120° apart. Vibrant but balanced — a common pattern for three-color compositions where each color has roughly equal weight on the wheel (though rarely equal perceptual weight)."),
            new("Trichromacy",
                "Human color vision uses three cone photoreceptor types (L/M/S, peaking around 564/534/420 nm). Every color you perceive is a relative activation of these three. Three-primary screens (RGB) work because matching three cone responses is sufficient to reproduce any visible color."),
            new("Tritanopia (tritan)",
                "Rare form of color vision deficiency (~0.01%) where the S cone is absent or shifted. Blue-green and yellow-pink pairs become ambiguous; red-green relationships stay relatively intact. Often acquired (medication, age) rather than congenital."),
            new("Value",
                "Munsell's term for how light or dark a color is, regardless of hue or chroma. Ranges 0 (black) to 10 (white) in the original notation. Survives in modern color spaces as the L axis of HSL, Lab and OKLCH."),
            new("WCAG",
                "Web Content Accessibility Guidelines. The web's standard for measuring whether text is legible against its background, graded by contrast ratio. AA is the practical floor (4.5:1 for body text); AAA is enhanced (7:1)."),
            new("WEIRD bias",
                "Henrich, Heine & Norenzayan's 2010 critique that most psychology research draws from Western, Educated, Industrialized, Rich, Democratic populations — one of the least representative samples of humanity. Findings on WEIRD samples do not generalize as cleanly as published abstracts often imply."),
            new("White point",
                "The chromaticity coordinate a color space treats as white. Web standards use D65 (a daylight illuminant at ~6500K). Print uses D50. Mismatched white points are why a photo color-corrected on a daylight-balanced monitor can look subtly off elsewhere."),
        };
    }
}
